#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"}] :question/id #uuid "515ab620-8513-55cc-8c88-61c0ad27aff2" :question/math-academy-id "e-2833" :question/problem "Which of the following terms describe the shape below?\n\n1. Parallelogram\n2. Rectangle\n3. Square\n\n![](images/d5/d578ca7af1f030dabb8e44a5a8fab90ce4bd21d002054fccdf2be2413a855a5c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of opposite congruent angles, so it is a parallelogram.\n- Since all the angles in this parallelogram are right, it is a rectangle.\n- Not all the sides of this rectangle are congruent, so it is not a square.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (parallelogram) and II (rectangle)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"}] :question/id #uuid "ee3a028c-93df-5e5a-bc06-4c286a5e1839" :question/math-academy-id "e-2834" :question/problem "Which of the following shapes is a trapezoid?\n\n![](images/6a/6a9f79e60fb5fca0ffee8fed594c19e946d3d6f02544f65ebd7e040e5cbf1ca0.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a trapezoid is a quadrilateral with exactly one pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid since it has one pair of opposite sides that are parallel.\n- The shape $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rhombus. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n- The shape $\\mathcal{R}$ is a kite. Its opposite sides are not parallel, therefore it is not a trapezoid.\n- The shape $\\mathcal{S}$ is a rectangle. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n\nTherefore, only shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals"}] :question/id #uuid "a7b82dde-36fc-582e-9ed6-fbfcfbf8b47c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2839" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. A rhombus is always a kite.\n2. A rhombus is always a parallelogram.\n3. A kite is always a parallelogram." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A rhombus fits this description, because all the sides in a rhombus are congruent.\n- Statement II is true. In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. The same is true for a rhombus. A rhombus is a specific type of parallelogram, where all the sides are congruent.\n- Statement III is false. In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. However, in a kite, it's only necessary that two pairs of congruent sides are adjacent to each other. Therefore, a kite is not always a parallelogram.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaeafc54-7b92-5062-9bdb-15d818ddc76e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109286} {:answer/id #uuid "e459090c-5c9d-5d00-95e2-d581d6447dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109287} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d9a4f1-68e6-5d47-8c82-5c0b4c9aa084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109288} {:answer/id #uuid "35964d8d-753e-5e39-9f1d-9b0c320a166c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109289} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd5188-95f9-5c2b-b965-cff6cf0912dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109290}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5fa196-47ec-5e14-99ea-eb0655130b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109285}] :question/id #uuid "599179be-ddc2-50ab-a431-64168ebbcf12" :question/math-academy-id "q-53000" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bffbc53c9e35da03655638ca40c5899bc5290cbadf3d14aef8e82585f14e004.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\n1. Kite\n2. Rectangle\n3. Trapezoid"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/titl